.TH GIT-TIME 1 "2026-11-19" "git-time 0.1.0" "Git Manual"
.SH NAME
git\-time \- RFC 3161 timestamp evidence for commits
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B git time [ \-C \fIDIR\fR ] \fIcommand\fR [ \fIarguments\fR ]
.PP
.B git time hook \fIevent\fR [ \fIrest\fR ... ]
.PP
.B git time seal [ \-\-range \fIRANGE\fR ] [ \-\-force ]
.PP
.B git time drop
.PP
.B git time verify [ \-\-range \fIRANGE\fR ] [ \-\-require\-sealed ] [ \-\-require\-signed ] [ \-\-json ] [ \fIcommit\fR ... ]
.PP
.B git time log [ \-\-range \fIRANGE\fR ] [ \-\-json ] [ \fIcommit\fR ... ]
.PP
.B git time install [ \-\-no\-tsa ]
.PP
.B git time uninstall [ \-\-force ]
.PP
.B git time doctor [ \-\-json ]
.PP
.B git time tsa \fIsubcommand\fR [ \fIarguments\fR ]
.PP
.B git time anchor \fIsubcommand\fR [ \fIarguments\fR ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B git time
seals commits with RFC 3161 timestamp tokens from one or more timestamp
authorities and stores the tokens inside the commit object as
.B X\-RFC3161\-*
trailers.
Sealing happens from git hooks, in the background, so an ordinary
.B git commit
needs no extra command.
.PP
A seal hashes a pre\-image of the commit, asks every configured authority for
a token over that hash, verifies each answer against the anchor pinned for its
authority, and rewrites the commit with the surviving tokens.
A signed commit is re\-signed over the new bytes.
A commit that cannot be sealed for any reason is left exactly as it was.
.PP
.B git time verify
judges the evidence and fails closed: a missing block, a bad imprint, a token
that does not chain to its pinned anchor, or a broken commit signature is a
failure with exit status 1.
.PP
The commit is only rewritten when it is not yet reachable from a
remote\-tracking ref.
A fast\-forwarded commit is left alone, because rewriting it would put the
branch ahead of its remote and force the next push.
.B git time seal \-\-force
rewrites such a commit when that is wanted.
.PP
An evidence block records what was hashed and which authorities answered.
It is evidence, not trust: whether the tokens are worth anything is decided at
verification time against the pinned anchors.
The format is specified in
.BR docs/format.md .
.SH COMMANDS
.SS hook
.B git time hook \fIevent\fR [ \fIrest\fR ... ]
.PP
The entrypoint the installed shims call.
.I event
is one of
.BR post\-commit ,
.BR post\-merge ,
.B pre\-commit
or
.BR pre\-merge\-commit .
Trailing arguments are passed through unchanged; git passes
.B 1
to
.B post\-merge
for a squash merge.
.PP
A merge never runs
.BR post\-commit ,
which is why both post hooks exist.
The two pre hooks do work only under
.BR "time.gate = pre\-commit" ;
they probe every configured authority over a dummy digest and refuse the
commit before any object is written when fewer than
.B time.required
answers arrive.
A pending drop short\-circuits the probe.
.PP
Shim failures are silent to git, which ignores a
.B post\-commit
status.
Under
.BR "time.on\-failure = fail"
the hook exits 3 instead.
.PP
Exits 0 on success and on every skip, 1 from a refusing gate, 3 on failure
under
.BR "time.on\-failure = fail" ,
and 2 for an unknown event.
.SS seal
.B git time seal [ \-\-range \fIRANGE\fR ] [ \-\-force ]
.PP
Seal HEAD, or every commit of
.I RANGE
oldest first.
Without arguments this is what the hook does, with the outcome printed.
A commit dropped with
.B git time drop
prints
.B "skipped (dropped)"
and is left alone.
.PP
The range form parks HEAD on each commit without moving the index or the
worktree, seals the commits that carry no matching evidence, and restores
HEAD.
Only a range that ends at the branch tip is sealed through the branch; other
members are sealed detached, and the printed object id is where the new
evidence lives.
Descendants of a rewritten member are not repointed.
.TP
.B \-\-range \fIRANGE\fR
A git rev\-range. Every commit in it is sealed, oldest first, in topological
order.
.TP
.B \-\-force
Re\-seal commits that already carry matching evidence, and seal commits that
were dropped on purpose.
.PP
Exits 0 on success and on a skip, 1 if a commit could not be sealed or no
authority answered, and 2 for a bad range or an unreadable configuration.
.SS drop
.B git time drop
.PP
Leave the next commit unsealed on purpose.
The marker survives the shell, so no environment variable has to be
remembered, and
.B verify
reports
.B "UNSEALED (dropped)"
instead of a plain unsealed commit.
Only a hook run that ends in a seal consumes the marker.
.PP
Exits 0, or 2 when the marker cannot be written.
.SS verify
.B git time verify [ \-\-range \fIRANGE\fR ] [ \-\-require\-sealed ] [ \-\-require\-signed ] [ \-\-json ] [ \fIcommit\fR ... ]
.PP
Judge the evidence of each commit once, without calling an authority.
The default target is HEAD.
Output is one line per commit: PASS or FAIL, the short object id, the verdict
and the verified quorum, followed by one indented line per authority answer
and per failing gate.
An answer line carries the verdict, the genTime and the signer's serial, so a
PASS shows who answered and when instead of only that a quorum was reached.
The anchor's SHA-256 is the one field left to `--json`, where it is there to be
compared rather than read; in the plain output the same long fingerprint on
every pass drowns the line.
Records are separated by a blank line.
Warnings are printed on a PASS too.
.PP
Read\-only, except that the signature check may leave one loose object behind
with
.BR "hash\-object \-w" .
No ref, index or worktree is touched.
.TP
.B \-\-range \fIRANGE\fR
A git rev\-range. Every commit in it is judged, newest first.
.TP
.B \-\-require\-sealed
Fail an unsealed commit.
This is a gate, not a verdict: the verdict stays
.BR unsealed .
.TP
.B \-\-require\-signed
Fail an unsigned commit.
This is a gate, not a verdict.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print the findings as one JSON document.
Each
.B per_tsa
entry carries
.BR serial
and
.B anchor
alongside the verdict.
.TP
.B \fIcommit\fR ...
Additional commits to judge, on top of any range.
An argument that does not name a commit is a usage error.
.PP
Exits 0 only when every commit passes, 1 on any evidence or gate failure, and
2 for a usage or configuration problem.
.SS log
.B git time log [ \-\-range \fIRANGE\fR ] [ \-\-json ] [ \fIcommit\fR ... ]
.PP
List the evidence of each commit, newest first, as one positional line: short
object id, authority ids, the genTime of every valid token, and the verdict.
An empty column is
.BR \- .
.PP
.B log
is a record, not a judgement: it exits 0 whenever it could read everything
and 1 only for a commit it could not read.
Use
.B verify
to gate CI.
The JSON form carries no
.BR pass ,
.B gates
or
.BR required ,
but its
.B per_tsa
entries keep
.BR serial
and
.BR anchor .
.TP
.B \-\-range \fIRANGE\fR
A git rev\-range. Every commit in it is listed, newest first.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print the evidence as one JSON document.
.TP
.B \fIcommit\fR ...
Additional commits to list.
.PP
Exits 0, 1 for a commit that could not be read, and 2 for a usage or
configuration problem.
.SS install
.B git time install [ \-\-no\-tsa ]
.PP
Wire the hooks into the current repository.
.B post\-commit
and
.B post\-merge
are always wired;
.B pre\-commit
and
.B pre\-merge\-commit
are wired only under
.BR "time.gate = pre\-commit" .
The hooks directory is the one git reports, so a
.B core.hooksPath
set by husky or the pre\-commit framework is honoured.
.PP
A foreign hook at any of those names is preserved as
.I name\fB.pre\-time\fR
(then
.B name.pre\-time.n)
and chained, never overwritten.
The shims run a preserved hook first and propagate its failure.
.PP
The authority set belongs to the repository, not to a flag: an existing
.B time.tsa.*
configuration is kept unchanged, and an empty one hands over to
.B git time tsa select
when stdin is a terminal.
A failed anchor fetch is a warning, not a failure; the authority stays
configured so
.B doctor
can name the gap.
.PP
The hooks resolve
.B git\-time
as a bare command through
.BR PATH .
The binary must be on
.B PATH
for any sealing to happen; otherwise the shims exit 0 and every commit stays
unsealed and silent.
.B install
warns when the binary is not found, and
.B doctor
reports it as a problem.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-tsa
Write no authority configuration; keep whatever is configured.
.PP
Exits 0, 1 when a chosen authority cannot be pinned, or 2 when no authority
is configured and stdin is not a terminal, or on a configuration error.
.SS uninstall
.B git time uninstall [ \-\-force ]
.PP
Remove every shim this tool owns, all four names included, and restore each
preserved hook by rename, so its bytes and mode come back exactly.
A hook that is not ours is left alone.
Running it twice succeeds.
.PP
A shim edited by hand is named and left in place.
.TP
.B \-\-force
Also replace a hand\-edited shim, keeping the edit under the next preserved
name next to the restored original.
When nothing was ever preserved, the edited shim stays where it is.
.PP
Exits 0, or 2 on a configuration problem.
.SS doctor
.B git time doctor [ \-\-json ]
.PP
Report, one fact per line, where the binary is, which hooks directory git
uses, the state of each of the four hooks
.B ( ours ,
.BR chained ,
.BR foreign ,
.BR absent ,
.BR not\-executable ,
.BR dangling ),
whether
.B time.gate
agrees with the installed pre hooks, how many authorities are configured, and
each anchor path with its SHA\-256 fingerprint.
Each problem is repeated as a
.B problem:
line.
.PP
A missing binary, no authority, a missing, unreadable or unparseable anchor,
a gate mismatch, an unknown gate value, a shim or preserved hook without its
execute bit, and a dangling symlink all exit 1.
A foreign or absent hook alone does not.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print the report as one JSON object. Its
.B problems
array decides the exit status.
.PP
Exits 0 when healthy, 1 on any problem, and 2 on a usage or configuration
error.
.SS tsa
.B git time tsa \fIsubcommand\fR [ \fIarguments\fR ]
.PP
The authority registry.
An authority is a
.B time.tsa.\fIid\fR
section of the local repository configuration.
An id is restricted to
.BR a\-z ,
.B 0\-9
and
.BR \- ;
when
.B \-\-id
is absent it is derived from the URL host, so
.B https://tsa.example.net/tsr
becomes
.BR tsa\-example\-net .
.SS "tsa add"
.B git time tsa add \fIurl\fR [ \-\-id \fIID\fR ] [ \-\-anchor \fIPATH\fR ] [ \-\-alg \fIsha256\fR|\fIsha512\fR ] [ \-\-policy \fIOID\fR ] [ \-\-timeout \fIDURATION\fR ] [ \-\-insecure\-http ]
.PP
Register an authority.
Every value is validated before the first key is written, so a refused add
leaves no half section behind.
An already configured id is refused.
A
.B http://
URL needs
.BR \-\-insecure\-http .
.TP
.B \-\-id \fIID\fR
The config id.
.TP
.B \-\-anchor \fIPATH\fR
The PEM bundle pinned for this id.
Defaults to
.B \fIanchors\-dir\fR/\fIid\fR.pem.
.TP
.B \-\-alg \fIsha256\fR|\fIsha512\fR
The imprint algorithm and the algorithm requested from the authority.
Default
.BR sha256 .
.TP
.B \-\-policy \fIOID\fR
A TSA policy OID to request.
.TP
.B \-\-timeout \fIDURATION\fR
Per\-attempt timeout. Default
.BR 6s .
.TP
.B \-\-insecure\-http
Accept a plain
.B http://
endpoint and warn that the transport is unprotected.
.PP
Exits 0, 1 for an existing id, and 2 for a bad URL, id, duration or policy.
.SS "tsa list"
.B git time tsa list [ \-\-json ]
.PP
List each configured authority with its URL, algorithm, anchor path,
fingerprint, anchor state and whether it is plain http.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print the listing as one JSON array.
.PP
Exits 0, or 2 on a configuration error.
.SS "tsa remove"
.B git time tsa remove \fIid\fR
.PP
Delete the whole
.B time.tsa.\fIid\fR
section. The anchor file is not removed.
.PP
Exits 0, 1 for an unknown id, and 2 for a bad id or configuration error.
.SS "tsa preset"
.B git time tsa preset list
.PP
Print the built\-in presets with their scope
.B ( default
or
.BR "opt\-in" ),
their
.B pinned
fingerprint or
.BR tofu ,
and whether the anchor is present.
Exits 0.
.PP
.B git time tsa preset add \fIname\fR
.PP
Configure one preset, anchor included, through the same fetch path
.B install
uses.
A root fingerprint that does not match the published value is a refusal, so a
pinned preset never accepts a substituted root.
A trust\-on\-first\-use preset prints the fetched fingerprint in full for
review.
.PP
Exits 0, 1 when no anchor could be pinned, and 2 for an unknown name or
configuration error.
.SS "tsa select"
.B git time tsa select [ \-\-interactive ] [ \-\-dry\-run ]
.PP
Choose interactively which preset authorities this repository uses, pin the
chosen anchors, and remove preset sections that were not chosen.
A section whose id is not a preset is untouched, so saving with nothing
selected narrows the configuration.
.PP
The list is a cursor interface:
.B space
ticks a row,
.B a
and
.B n
take or clear all,
.B j
and
.B k
or the arrow keys move,
.B enter
saves,
.B q
aborts.
Typed input
.B ( 1,3\-4 ,
.BR all ,
.BR none )
still applies.
.PP
Without a terminal the command refuses rather than hanging.
.TP
.B \-\-interactive
Prompt even when stdin is not a terminal, so a piped answer works.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Print what would be enabled and disabled, change nothing.
.PP
Exits 0, 1 when a chosen anchor cannot be pinned, and 2 without a terminal,
after three unparseable answers, or on a configuration error.
.SS anchor
.B git time anchor \fIsubcommand\fR [ \fIarguments\fR ]
.PP
Work with pinned anchor certificates.
The first certificate of a bundle is the trust anchor for its authority.
.SS "anchor fetch"
.B git time anchor fetch \fIurl\fR \-\-id \fIid\fR [ \-\-insecure\-http ]
.PP
Fetch a PEM, DER or PKCS#7 bundle and pin it for
.BR id .
For a preset, its issuer chain is appended behind the root.
The bundle is parsed before it replaces anything, so a garbled fetch leaves a
working anchor intact.
The target is the configured
.B time.tsa.\fIid\fR.anchor
when set, else the anchors directory.
.TP
.B \-\-id \fIid\fR
The authority the bundle belongs to. Required.
.TP
.B \-\-insecure\-http
Accept a plain
.B http://
URL.
.PP
Exits 0, 1 for an unusable bundle, and 2 for a bad id, URL or configuration
error.
A bundle for an id without a section is written and warned about as an
orphan.
.SS "anchor list"
.B git time anchor list [ \-\-json ]
.PP
Name each configured anchor with its SHA\-256 fingerprint and
.BR notAfter .
A past
.B notAfter
is a cheap stale signal.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print the listing as one JSON array.
.PP
Exits 0, or 2 on a configuration error.
.SS "anchor pin"
.B git time anchor pin [ \-\-check ]
.PP
Write
.B time.anchors\-lock
in the repository root, one
.I id fingerprint
line per configured authority, or compare the anchors against it.
.TP
.B \-\-check
Compare instead of writing, naming every missing, unparseable, changed and
unrecorded id.
.PP
Exits 0, 1 on a mismatch, an unreadable lock or an unusable anchor, and 2 on a
configuration error.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-C \fIDIR\fR
Run in the repository at
.I DIR
instead of the current directory.
Accepted by every command.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print help and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version with the copyright line, the licence and the source URL, then
exit.
.SH "X\-RFC3161 EVIDENCE"
A sealed commit carries a trailing message paragraph of
.B X\-RFC3161\-
lines:
.B Imprint
(what was hashed),
.B Object
(provenance),
.B Token
(one per authority that answered) and
.B Fail
(one per authority that was asked and did not deliver).
Tokens are zstd\-compressed and base64url\-encoded.
The block travels inside the object and is covered by the commit signature.
.PP
The block is evidence, not trust.
Verification recomputes the pre\-image, compares the imprint in constant time,
and judges every token on its own imprint algorithm and id against the anchor
pinned for that id.
.PP
The format, the pre\-image rules, the budget and the mandatory verifier checks
are specified in
.BR docs/format.md .
.SH CONFIGURATION
All keys are git configuration, normally repository\-local.
An unknown value for a key with a fixed vocabulary is an error with exit 2,
never a silent fallback to the default.
Durations accept
.BR 500ms ,
.B 10s ,
.B 3m
and bare seconds.
A relative anchor path is resolved against the repository;
.B ~/
is resolved against
.BR HOME .
.PP
An authority whose anchor bundle does not load is left out of the seal and
recorded as a
.B Fail
for its id, unless failures are not recorded.
Only when no configured authority has a usable anchor does the seal refuse.
.TP
.B time.required
Default
.BR 1 .
Distinct authorities that must answer for a seal to count as sealed.
.B 0
makes the seal best effort.
.TP
.B time.deadline
Default
.BR 10s .
Hard bound on the whole fan\-out, not on one authority.
.TP
.B time.on\-failure
Default
.BR warn .
.B warn
prints and exits 0;
.B fail
prints and exits 3.
.TP
.B time.gate
Default
.BR post\-commit .
.B post\-commit
seals after the object exists;
.B pre\-commit
probes the authorities first and refuses the commit on a short quorum.
.TP
.B time.store
Default
.BR trailer .
Where evidence goes:
.B trailer
(inside the object),
.B note
.B ( refs/notes/rfc3161 )
or
.BR both .
.TP
.B time.seal\-during\-rebase
Default
.BR all .
Which rewritten commits a rebase seals:
.BR all ,
only the
.B tip
with the exact command for the rest printed once, or
.B off
with the
.B seal \-\-range
hint.
.TP
.B time.record\-failures
Default
.BR true .
Whether a failed authority leaves an
.B X\-RFC3161\-Fail
line.
.TP
.B time.enabled
Default
.BR true .
Whether the hook seals at all.
.TP
.B time.max\-trailer\-bytes
Default
.BR 65536 .
Budget for the trailer block.
A breach drops the largest token and records
.BR oversized .
.TP
.B time.tsa.\fIid\fR.url
The endpoint requests are posted to.
Required for every authority.
.TP
.B time.tsa.\fIid\fR.anchor
The pinned PEM bundle tokens are judged against.
Required for every authority.
.TP
.B time.tsa.\fIid\fR.alg
Default
.BR sha256 .
Imprint and request algorithm:
.B sha256
or
.BR sha512 .
.TP
.B time.tsa.\fIid\fR.policy
Unset by default.
A policy OID to request, validated before it is written.
.TP
.B time.tsa.\fIid\fR.timeout
Default
.BR 6s .
Per\-attempt timeout.
.TP
.B time.tsa.\fIid\fR.insecure\-http
Default
.BR false .
Records that a plain
.B http://
endpoint was accepted on purpose.
All such authorities share one warning line at seal time.
.PP
.B time.anchors\-lock
is a file, not a key.
It records reviewed fingerprints but is not a trust store: verification keeps
judging against the pinned bundles, so deleting the lock changes no verdict.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I anchors\-dir
The pinned bundles, by default
.B $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/git\-time/anchors
or
.BR ~/.config/git\-time/anchors .
The first certificate of a bundle is the anchor.
.TP
.B time.anchors\-lock
In the repository root, written by
.BR "anchor pin" .
One
.I id fingerprint
line per configured authority.
.TP
.I gitdir\fB/time\-skip
The marker written by
.BR drop .
It is removed by the next hook run that seals.
.TP
.I gitdir\fB/time\-dropped
One dropped object id per line, so
.B seal
can report a deliberate drop.
Capped at 200 entries and pruned as commits disappear.
.TP
.B hooks\-dir/{post\-commit,post\-merge,pre\-commit,pre\-merge\-commit}
The shims.
Each runs a preserved hook first, then
.BR "git\-time hook" .
.TP
.I hooks\-dir\fB/\fIname\fB.pre\-time
The foreign hooks displaced by
.BR install ,
restored by
.BR uninstall .
Further displacements take
.B name.pre\-time.n\&.
.TP
.I rebase\-dir\fB/time\-hinted
Set once per rebase when
.B time.seal\-during\-rebase = off
so the hint is printed once.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.TP
.B 0
Success.
A skip is a success.
.TP
.B 1
Evidence or seal failure, and the verdict of a refusing gate.
.TP
.B 2
Usage or configuration problem.
.TP
.B 3
Only from
.BR hook ,
and only under
.BR "time.on\-failure = fail" .
git ignores a
.B post\-commit
status; a wrapper does not.
.SH EXAMPLES
Set a repository up:
.PP
.B git time tsa select
.PP
.B git time install
.PP
.B git commit \-m \&"add entry point\&"
.PP
Judge the history in CI:
.PP
.B git time verify \-\-range origin/main..HEAD \-\-require\-sealed
.PP
Seal a range after the fact, and keep the printed ids:
.PP
.B git time seal \-\-range HEAD~5..HEAD
.PP
Review anchors, then catch later changes:
.PP
.B git time anchor pin
.PP
.B git time anchor pin \-\-check
.PP
Skip one commit on purpose:
.PP
.B git time drop
.PP
.B git commit \-m \&"wip\&"
.SH "SECURITY CONSIDERATIONS"
The pinned anchors are the trust store.
A token is only as good as the bundle pinned under its id, so pin deliberately
and review a changed fingerprint out of band against the authority's own
publication.
The lock is a review artifact, not a trust store.
.PP
Plain
.B http://
presets keep full token integrity, because the CMS signature is checked
against the pinned root.
Transport only exposes the imprint and lets an attacker block the authority.
That is why every insecure authority warns at seal time.
.PP
Published history is never rewritten by the hook.
Rewriting a commit a remote already has moves the branch ahead of it and can
force the next push.
Sealing it is an explicit
.B seal \-\-force
decision.
.PP
The hooks resolve
.B git\-time
through
.BR PATH ,
with git's environment and no shell aliases.
Whichever binary comes first on
.B PATH
is what runs at commit time.
.PP
Verification fails closed, and
.B verify
exits 0 only when every commit passes.
A token must chain to its anchor at
.BR genTime ,
must carry a critical extended key usage of exactly
.B id\-kp\-timeStamping
and nothing else (RFC 3161, section 2.3), and must lie in the accepted
genTime window.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR git\-commit (1),
.BR git\-hooks (5),
RFC 3161,
RFC 3161 section 2.3,
RFC 5280.
